==================================================
30. RESEARCH REPORT USAGE
==================================================

Two long research reports are provided in /research.

They are REFERENCE MATERIAL, not implementation specifications.

Do not treat a claim as established merely because one report states it confidently.

The reports contain:
- primary-source excerpts
- patent interpretation
- technical hypotheses
- implementation suggestions
- speculative parameter values
- community reports
- proposed experiments

Some details conflict between the two reports, and some plausible engineering hypotheses are presented with more certainty than the underlying evidence warrants.

AUTHORITY ORDER:

1. This project prompt
2. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/EVIDENCE_AND_HYPOTHESES.md
3. The supplied research reports
4. Your own inference

When a research report conflicts with the project specification, FOLLOW THE PROJECT SPECIFICATION.

When the reports disagree with each other:
- do not silently choose one;
- identify the disagreement;
- prefer primary-source evidence;
- otherwise preserve the uncertainty as a configurable experiment.

Especially DO NOT hardcode as historical facts any unsupported values for:
- target-note hysteresis
- note-confirmation period count
- voiced-confidence hold duration
- Tracking epsilon
- Humanize onset/ramp timing
- Retune Speed -> tau mapping
- cycle-splice crossfade duration
- exact EFX3 latency

If one of these values is useful as an engineering starting point, place it behind a developer parameter and document:

    Evidence status: EXPERIMENTAL

The research reports are most useful for:
- understanding the reasoning behind the architecture
- consulting patent equations
- locating primary sources
- identifying alternative hypotheses
- designing tests
- understanding why certain modern DSP approaches are intentionally excluded

They must not override the project's epistemic discipline.